<Guide lang="en" slug="building-on-your-own-land-building-a-pergola" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="Rather have a pergola built?" ctaText="No time or inclination to build it yourself? Tell us what you want and send a photo of the spot. We handle everything from notification to installation.">
  <h2 id="first">Check first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Is it allowed?</strong> An open pergola without a solid roof is usually easy to arrange, often with a notification to the town. With a solid roof it becomes a canopy, with stricter rules. Read <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/carport-and-pergola/">carport, canopy and pergola</a> first.</li>
    <li><strong>Live in a complex?</strong> Ask the community for consent before you buy anything.</li>
    <li><strong>The spot</strong>: see where the sun is in the afternoon and evening, where the wind comes from and whether pipes or cables run underground (water, drainage, power to the pool).</li>
    <li><strong>The size</strong>: a common size is 3 × 4 to 4 × 5 metres, with about 2.40 metres of headroom. Keep the span between wooden posts under 3 to 3.5 metres, or the beams must be heavier.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="material">Wood, aluminium or steel?</h2>
  <table class="prices plain">
    <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pressure-treated pine (<em>madera tratada en autoclave</em>)</td><td>The most common, sold everywhere and easy to work. Treat it every few years.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Douglas fir, larch or tropical hardwood</td><td>Lasts longer and looks better, but is heavier and harder to cut and drill.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aluminium kit</td><td>Almost no maintenance. You can assemble it yourself, but you are tied to the kit sizes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Steel</td><td>Strong and slim, but welding is skilled work. Near the coast, only galvanised or powder-coated.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>This guide assumes a freestanding wooden pergola on four or six posts.</p>

  <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
  <p><strong>Materials</strong> (handy to take the Spanish words to the DIY store or <em>almacén de maderas</em>):</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Posts of 9 × 9 or 12 × 12 cm (<em>postes</em>)</li>
    <li>Main beams on the posts, for example 7 × 17 cm or heavier (<em>vigas</em>)</li>
    <li>Cross beams or slats on top (<em>travesaños</em> or <em>listones</em>)</li>
    <li>Galvanised post anchors to set in the concrete (<em>anclajes para poste</em>)</li>
    <li>Concrete or bags of rapid-set concrete (<em>hormigón</em>)</li>
    <li>M10 or M12 coach bolts with washers, and stainless screws (<em>tornillería inoxidable</em>)</li>
    <li>Wood stain or oil with UV protection and a woodworm treatment (<em>carcoma</em>)</li>
    <li>For shade: reed matting (<em>cañizo</em>), shade cloth (<em>malla de sombreo</em>) or climbing plants</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Tools</strong>: tape measure, string and pegs, spirit level (ideally a long one or a laser level), spade or post-hole auger, circular saw, cordless drill with long wood bits, socket set, two sturdy ladders and a second person.</p>

  <h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Set out.</strong> Mark the corners with pegs and string. Check it is square by measuring both diagonals: they must be equal.</li>
    <li><strong>Dig the holes.</strong> One hole per post of about 40 × 40 cm and 50 to 60 cm deep. In the rocky Axarquía soil you sometimes need a breaker; you can hire one.</li>
    <li><strong>Pour the footings.</strong> Fill the holes with concrete and set the post anchors in it, exactly on the string line and at the same height. Let the concrete cure for at least a few days, covered in summer heat and wetted now and then.</li>
    <li><strong>Stand the posts.</strong> Fit the posts in the anchors, keep them plumb on two sides with a level and brace them temporarily. Bolt them down.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut to height.</strong> Mark the height on all posts with a (laser) level and cut them to the same length. Give the top a slight fall if something waterproof may go on later.</li>
    <li><strong>Fit the main beams.</strong> Set the beams on or against the posts and fix them with through coach bolts. Pre-drill and use washers.</li>
    <li><strong>Lay the cross beams.</strong> Lay cross beams or slats every 40 to 60 cm on the main beams and screw them down. The closer, the more shade.</li>
    <li><strong>Brace against the wind.</strong> Fit diagonal knee braces between posts and beams. They make the pergola rigid, which is no luxury in the Axarquía wind.</li>
    <li><strong>Finish.</strong> Treat all the wood, including the cut ends. Fix the reed matting or shade cloth, or plant climbers such as wisteria, bougainvillea or grapes at the base.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="wall">Against the wall or on a roof terrace</h2>
  <p>A pergola that rests on the house on one side hangs from a wall plate fixed with heavy anchors. In the old, sometimes crumbly walls of village houses and fincas, ordinary plugs will not hold; use chemical anchors. A pergola against the wall is more likely to count as an extension of the house, and in a complex it changes the look of the building.</p>
  <p>On a roof terrace it is harder still: the roof must carry the weight and wind load, and every hole in the membrane is a potential leak. Always leave that to a professional.</p>

  <h2 id="andalusia">What is different in Andalusia</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sun</strong>: UV turns untreated wood grey and cracked within a few years. Treat it every two to three years.</li>
    <li><strong>Wind</strong>: especially on hilltops and in autumn it can blow hard. A taut shade cloth catches a lot of wind; take it down in a storm.</li>
    <li><strong>Woodworm and termites</strong>: keep the wood at least a few centimetres off the ground, as a post anchor does, and check it every year.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy rain</strong>: short but intense in winter. Do not let water stand on beams and keep the post bases dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Salt</strong>: near the coast, ordinary bolts and screws rust quickly. Use stainless steel or hot-dip galvanised.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="professional">When to call in a professional</h2>
  <p>A simple, freestanding wooden pergola is a good DIY job. Leave it to a professional if:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>you want a louvred, solid or glass roof;</li>
    <li>the pergola goes on a roof terrace or against an old wall;</li>
    <li>it is larger than about 4 × 5 metres, or stands on a steep slope;</li>
    <li>it needs lighting, power or an outdoor kitchen underneath;</li>
    <li>you need a permit and drawings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Want the pergola built for you, or just the footings and heavy work done?</strong> <a href="/en/request/?dienst=renovatie">Get in touch →</a></p>
  <p>Back to the overview: <a href="/en/guides/building-on-your-own-land-spain/">building on your own land</a>.</p>
</Guide>
